<h1>Testing</h1>
<p>see foam/core/saf/test/SAFClusterTest.  A test which mimics a two instance cluster. Manipulates SAFConfig and HealthStatus to test that DAO operations are stored and eventually delivered to the 'other' instance.</p>

<h1>Receiving</h1>
<p>SAF has no separate server component. Every instance that includes
  the saf deployment both sends and receives. On the receiving side,
  the foam3 socket server routes incoming messages to the
  <b>safBroadcastReceiverDAO</b> CSpec, which applies each entry to the
  target DAO's MDAO.</p>

<h2>How an entry reaches the receiver</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Socket server. The foam3 <b>socketServer</b> CSpec starts at boot
    (lazy:false). It listens on the <b>SocketServer</b> port, which is
    the http port + 3 (foam/box/socket/ports.jrl). The build's -W option
    sets the http port, so -W8698 means the socket server listens on 8701.</li>
  <li>Routing. SocketRouter takes the service name from the message's
    SubBoxMessage and looks up the CSpec with that name. It sends the
    message through SessionServerBox, which applies session
    authentication.</li>
  <li>Receiver CSpec. <b>safBroadcastReceiverDAO</b> is an EasyDAO with
    serve:true, so the router can reach it, and with saf and ruler both
    false. It wraps SAFBroadcastReceiverDAO over a NullDAO.</li>
  <li>Apply. SAFBroadcastReceiverDAO.put_ looks up the DAO named by the
    entry's cSpecName, takes its innermost DAO with
    cmd_(DAO.LAST_CMD), and calls put_ or remove_ on it according to the
    entry's dop. This skips the rules, authorization and the
    SAFBroadcastDAO above the MDAO, so a received entry is never
    forwarded on again.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How the sender connects to it</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Who to send to. SAFManager.getBroadcastIds selects every enabled
    SAFConfig other than the instance's own, and createClient makes one
    SAFClientDAO for each. The instance finds its own SAFConfig by the
    <b>hostname</b> system property; when that is 'localhost' it uses
    the user.name property instead.</li>
  <li>Service name. SAFClientDAO sends to the service
    <b>safBroadcastReceiverDAO</b>.</li>
  <li>Address. SAFSupport.getSocketClientBox looks up the SAFConfig id in
    hostDAO (hosts.jrl) to get the address. It uses the SAFConfig port,
    and falls back to the SocketServer port only when that port is 0.
    The fallback uses the sender's own SocketServer port, so it only
    works when both instances use the same -W. On a hostDAO miss the id
    is used verbatim as the hostname with no warning, so an absent or
    unloaded hosts.jrl can silently send to the wrong host (for example
    a 127.0.0.1 self-loop, where the sender's own safBroadcastReceiverDAO
    applies the entries it just emitted and the peer receives nothing).</li>
  <li>Login. SAFSupport.getBroadcastClientDAO wraps the socket box in a
    SessionClientBox using the receiver SAFConfig's sessionId, or the
    session of the sending context when there is one. That session must
    exist on the receiver (sessionsinternal.jrl), along with the user it
    belongs to (users.jrl).</li>
  <li>Config rows. Each instance needs a safconfigs.jrl row for every
    peer.</li>
</ul>
